---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
import { breadcrumbList, howToSchema } from '../lib/seo';

const processSteps = [
  {
    name: 'Discovery & scope',
    text: 'Goals, audience, competitors, platform constraints and success measures. You leave with a written scope, not a vibe.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Structure & design',
    text: 'Information architecture, key templates and a visual system that supports conversion — brand-first without clutter.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Build',
    text: 'Implementation on the agreed stack with content modelling, forms, SEO foundations and responsive QA.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Launch',
    text: 'Redirects (if needed), analytics events, final content check and go-live support.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Support',
    text: 'Optional maintenance for updates, monitoring and small improvements after launch.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Our Process | Website Development Perth"
  description="A five-step website delivery process for Perth businesses — discovery, structure, build, launch and support, with written scope and checkpoints."
  jsonLd={[
    breadcrumbList([
      { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
      { name: 'Process', path: '/process/' },
    ]),
    howToSchema({
      name: 'How Website Development Perth delivers a website',
      description:
        'A five-step website delivery process for Perth businesses: discovery and scope, structure and design, build, launch and optional support.',
      steps: processSteps,
    }),
  ]}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Home', href: '/' }, { label: 'Our Process' }]} />
      <h1>Our process</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Reduce risk with a delivery path you can follow — checkpoints that keep decisions moving, and a scope that says what is in and what is not.
      </p>
      <dl class="glance">
        <div>
          <dt>Typical start</dt>
          <dd>2–4 weeks after an agreed scope</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Custom / service site</dt>
          <dd>Usually 6–10 weeks once content is moving</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Redesign</dt>
          <dd>Often 6–12 weeks for a typical SME site</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Landing page</dt>
          <dd>1–3 weeks when offer and assets are ready</dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" style="padding-top: 0">
    <div class="container prose">
      <p>
        Timelines stretch when copy, photography or committee review arrives late — not because we “add weeks for quality”. For the calendar view, read <a href="/blog/website-project-timeline/">how long a website project takes</a>. For what drives price, see <a href="/blog/website-cost-perth/">website cost in Perth</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>1. Discovery &amp; scope</h2>
      <p>
        We start with the job: who you sell to, what the site must do, which platform your team can actually run, and how you will know it worked (enquiries, bookings, orders — not “a modern look”).
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>We need from you:</strong> examples you like and dislike, access to the current site and analytics if they exist, and an honest note on who approves copy.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>You leave with:</strong> a written scope, assumptions (content supplied or drafted with us), and a platform recommendation we can defend. No moodboard-as-contract.
      </p>

      <h2>2. Structure &amp; design</h2>
      <p>
        Information architecture first: services, proof, locations, contact. Then key templates and a visual system that can survive the next six pages you add after launch.
      </p>
      <p>
        Feedback is timeboxed after structure and after visual design so colour debates do not arrive on an unsettled sitemap. Brand-first does not mean clutter.
      </p>

      <h2>3. Build</h2>
      <p>
        Implementation on the agreed stack — WordPress, Webflow, Shopify, WooCommerce, Drupal, or custom — with content modelling, forms, SEO foundations (titles, canonicals, sensible URLs) and QA on real phones.
      </p>
      <p>
        Integrations are wired for the paths you will actually use (inbox, CRM, booking). We would rather ship one reliable enquiry path than five half-connected tools.
      </p>

      <h2>4. Launch</h2>
      <p>
        Staging cutover, not “edit live and hope”. If you already have a site, redirects, analytics events and a final content check are part of go-live — not a favour afterwards. DNS and email records are planned so the website move does not take the inbox down.
      </p>
      <p>
        You get a short watch window for critical fixes. Training sits here when the CMS is meant to be used by your team.
      </p>

      <h2>5. Support</h2>
      <p>
        Optional <a href="/services/website-maintenance/">maintenance</a>: updates, backups, monitoring and a monthly allowance for small fixes. New sections, redesigns and campaign pages stay as quoted project work so the care plan remains predictable.
      </p>

      <h2>Where projects stall</h2>
      <p>
        Missing copy, photography after UI is locked, extra stakeholders appearing in week six, and “can we just add a shop” mid-build. Discovery is designed to surface those risks while they are still cheap.
      </p>
      <p>
        Ready to start? <a href="/contact/">Send the brief</a> — goals, current site, and constraints are enough.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <CtaBand />
</BaseLayout>
